1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:02,714
Shrink the U.S. government
down to one household.

2
00:00:03,094 --> 00:00:06,325
It brings home five thousand
four hundred dollars a month.

3
00:00:06,705 --> 00:00:09,040
It spends about seven
thousand four hundred.

4
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:12,360
Let's sit at its kitchen table
and see where the gap goes.

5
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:15,048
Why does this matter outside Washington?

6
00:00:15,528 --> 00:00:17,560
Because when this household borrows,

7
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:21,481
the rate it pays is the starting
point for car loans and mortgages.

8
00:00:21,861 --> 00:00:26,180
By the end, you'll know which bill is
growing fastest, why it keeps growing,

9
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:29,539
and the three ways a country
can close a gap like this.

10
00:00:30,269 --> 00:00:33,579
I'm Leo, the cartoon roommate who
keeps the budget on the fridge.

11
00:00:33,959 --> 00:00:35,520
One rule in this kitchen:

12
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:40,503
one dollar a month here equals one
billion dollars a year in the real budget.

13
00:00:40,883 --> 00:00:45,382
These are real Treasury numbers, for
the year to September twenty twenty-six.

14
00:00:45,762 --> 00:00:48,592
And before we open a single
bill, here's my warning.

15
00:00:48,972 --> 00:00:52,845
A country isn't a family, but the
interest bill works the same way.

16
00:00:53,225 --> 00:00:54,475
Remember that sentence.

17
00:00:54,855 --> 00:00:56,807
We'll test it before we leave this table.

18
00:00:57,523 --> 00:00:58,323
Payday.

19
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:01,426
Five thousand four hundred
dollars lands on the table.

20
00:01:01,806 --> 00:01:04,178
Most of it comes from
three kinds of taxes.

21
00:01:04,558 --> 00:01:06,131
Taxes on people's paychecks.

22
00:01:06,511 --> 00:01:07,989
Taxes on company profits.

23
00:01:08,369 --> 00:01:12,719
And payroll taxes, the ones that pay
for Social Security and Medicare.

24
00:01:13,099 --> 00:01:15,115
That's the income side, and it's simple.

25
00:01:15,495 --> 00:01:17,800
The spending side is
where it gets interesting,

26
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,322
so I put every bill in its own
envelope, and I saved one for last.

27
00:01:23,052 --> 00:01:26,308
Envelope one is the fattest
of all: Social Security.

28
00:01:26,688 --> 00:01:31,329
These are the monthly checks for retired
people and people with disabilities.

29
00:01:31,709 --> 00:01:33,947
About one thousand six
hundred seventy dollars.

30
00:01:34,696 --> 00:01:38,296
Envelope two: Medicare, health
insurance for older Americans.

31
00:01:38,676 --> 00:01:40,528
About one thousand seventy dollars.

32
00:01:40,908 --> 00:01:43,894
Envelope three: other health
programs, like Medicaid.

33
00:01:44,274 --> 00:01:46,303
About one thousand twenty-five dollars.

34
00:01:46,683 --> 00:01:48,940
Three envelopes, and
we've already spent more

35
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:50,940
than three thousand seven hundred dollars.

36
00:01:52,086 --> 00:01:53,444
Envelope four: defense.

37
00:01:53,824 --> 00:01:55,091
The entire military.

38
00:01:55,471 --> 00:01:57,294
Ships, planes, salaries, bases.

39
00:01:57,674 --> 00:01:59,378
Nine hundred sixty-five dollars.

40
00:01:59,758 --> 00:02:01,134
Now look at envelope five.

41
00:02:01,514 --> 00:02:02,314
No logo.

42
00:02:02,627 --> 00:02:03,427
No picture.

43
00:02:03,795 --> 00:02:05,324
Just one word: interest.

44
00:02:05,704 --> 00:02:07,483
About one thousand fifty dollars.

45
00:02:07,863 --> 00:02:10,600
That makes it the third-biggest
bill on this table,

46
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,202
after Social Security and Medicare.

47
00:02:13,582 --> 00:02:14,476
Read that again.

48
00:02:14,856 --> 00:02:19,045
This household pays its lenders more
than it pays for its whole military.

49
00:02:19,425 --> 00:02:23,760
Interest alone eats about one in every
five dollars that comes in the door.

50
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:25,224
And it buys nothing.

51
00:02:25,604 --> 00:02:27,018
No road, no check, no ship.

52
00:02:27,398 --> 00:02:29,779
It's just the price of
money borrowed in the past.

53
00:02:30,506 --> 00:02:32,680
One more envelope holds everything else.

54
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:36,480
Veterans, roads, food aid,
science, national parks,

55
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:38,930
the courts, and hundreds
of smaller things.

56
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:41,167
About one thousand six hundred dollars.

57
00:02:41,736 --> 00:02:43,158
Now add up all the envelopes.

58
00:02:43,538 --> 00:02:46,016
About seven thousand
four hundred dollars out.

59
00:02:46,396 --> 00:02:47,942
Five thousand four hundred in.

60
00:02:48,322 --> 00:02:52,715
So every single month, this household
is about two thousand dollars short.

61
00:02:53,095 --> 00:02:55,397
And that two thousand
dollars goes on the loan.

62
00:02:56,127 --> 00:02:57,556
Let's open the loan statement.

63
00:02:57,921 --> 00:03:00,495
This household didn't
start borrowing last month.

64
00:03:00,875 --> 00:03:03,045
It has been adding to
this loan for decades.

65
00:03:03,424 --> 00:03:05,960
The balance it owes to outside lenders is

66
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:08,521
about three hundred
ninety thousand dollars.

67
00:03:08,901 --> 00:03:11,620
In the real world, that's
the debt held by the public:

68
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:14,063
about thirty-two trillion dollars.

69
00:03:14,443 --> 00:03:17,020
One fair note: this is
not an expensive loan.

70
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,586
On average, it costs only about three
to three and a half percent a year.

71
00:03:21,966 --> 00:03:25,540
There's also money the government
owes to its own trust funds,

72
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,171
like Social Security's.

73
00:03:27,551 --> 00:03:31,936
We'll leave that part out, because that
interest goes from one pocket to another.

74
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,289
Three hundred ninety thousand
dollars sounds like just a number.

75
00:03:36,669 --> 00:03:38,431
So let's put it next to the paycheck.

76
00:03:38,795 --> 00:03:40,437
It's about six years of income.

77
00:03:40,817 --> 00:03:44,100
Every dollar this household
earns, for six full years,

78
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:48,737
with nothing for food or rent or
anything else, just to clear the loan.

79
00:03:49,117 --> 00:03:51,586
And it's still adding two
thousand dollars a month.

80
00:03:52,316 --> 00:03:53,537
Who is holding this loan?

81
00:03:54,017 --> 00:03:54,914
Not one bank.

82
00:03:55,294 --> 00:03:56,884
Americans with savings bonds.

83
00:03:57,264 --> 00:03:58,133
Pension funds.

84
00:03:58,513 --> 00:04:00,116
Banks and insurance companies.

85
00:04:00,496 --> 00:04:01,541
Foreign governments.

86
00:04:01,921 --> 00:04:05,027
Even America's own central
bank, the Federal Reserve.

87
00:04:05,407 --> 00:04:09,569
When this household borrows, it sells
them a promise called a Treasury bond.

88
00:04:09,949 --> 00:04:13,242
Lend me money now, and I'll pay
you interest, then pay you back.

89
00:04:13,972 --> 00:04:17,920
Now, if a real family had a loan
this big and kept adding to it,

90
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:19,542
the bank would stop lending.

91
00:04:19,922 --> 00:04:23,817
So why do lenders around the world
keep saying yes to this household?

92
00:04:24,297 --> 00:04:25,407
Hold that question.

93
00:04:25,787 --> 00:04:26,825
We'll answer it soon.

94
00:04:27,205 --> 00:04:30,799
First, I want to show you something
strange inside envelope five.

95
00:04:31,529 --> 00:04:32,497
Envelope five.

96
00:04:32,877 --> 00:04:33,677
Interest.

97
00:04:33,916 --> 00:04:35,695
About one thousand fifty dollars.

98
00:04:36,075 --> 00:04:37,694
Watch what happens when I open it.

99
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:39,698
Inside, there's another envelope.

100
00:04:40,078 --> 00:04:41,119
It says: borrowed.

101
00:04:41,499 --> 00:04:42,299
Here's why.

102
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:44,913
The monthly gap is about
two thousand dollars.

103
00:04:45,293 --> 00:04:48,214
More than half of that gap
is the interest bill itself.

104
00:04:48,594 --> 00:04:52,930
So this household borrows money to pay
interest on money it borrowed before.

105
00:04:53,310 --> 00:04:55,146
And that new money needs interest too.

106
00:04:55,526 --> 00:04:59,303
So inside the borrowed envelope,
there's another interest envelope.

107
00:04:59,683 --> 00:05:02,270
And inside that one,
another borrowed envelope.

108
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:04,932
Interest, inside borrowed,
inside interest.

109
00:05:05,662 --> 00:05:08,443
So why did envelope five
get so fat, so fast?

110
00:05:08,923 --> 00:05:10,618
Part of it is the size of the loan.

111
00:05:10,998 --> 00:05:12,393
But the rate matters a lot.

112
00:05:12,773 --> 00:05:15,670
A few years ago, this household
borrowed very cheaply.

113
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:17,336
In early twenty twenty-two,

114
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:21,449
the average rate on its debt was
about one and a half percent.

115
00:05:21,829 --> 00:05:25,896
By September twenty twenty-six,
it was about three and a half percent.

116
00:05:26,276 --> 00:05:29,287
And in just five years, the
interest bill roughly tripled.

117
00:05:29,667 --> 00:05:31,870
In our kitchen, it went
from about three hundred

118
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:35,347
fifty dollars a month to
about one thousand fifty.

119
00:05:35,727 --> 00:05:36,832
And it's still growing.

120
00:05:37,562 --> 00:05:39,423
How can a new rate change an old debt?

121
00:05:39,903 --> 00:05:41,658
Because the balance isn't one loan.

122
00:05:42,038 --> 00:05:44,087
It's many loans, and they keep coming due.

123
00:05:44,467 --> 00:05:45,447
Some last weeks.

124
00:05:45,827 --> 00:05:46,970
Some last decades.

125
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:48,874
They don't come due all at once.

126
00:05:49,149 --> 00:05:50,702
They roll over a few at a time.

127
00:05:51,082 --> 00:05:56,038
Every time an old, cheap loan comes due,
it's replaced with a more expensive one.

128
00:05:56,418 --> 00:06:01,741
In late October twenty twenty-six, new
ten-year loans cost close to five percent.

129
00:06:02,121 --> 00:06:04,870
Even short-term borrowing
cost about four percent.

130
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:08,640
So the average rate keeps
climbing as long as new

131
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:11,560
loans cost more than the
old ones they replace,

132
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:15,263
even if the household never
borrows one more dollar.

133
00:06:15,643 --> 00:06:19,504
That's the part of the story that
works exactly like a family's loan.

134
00:06:19,884 --> 00:06:20,986
Rate times balance.

135
00:06:21,366 --> 00:06:23,791
And it's the one bill
this household can't skip.

136
00:06:24,171 --> 00:06:26,726
But this is where the family
story starts to break.

137
00:06:27,106 --> 00:06:30,319
Because a family can't do three
things this household can.

138
00:06:31,049 --> 00:06:33,059
Thing one: it borrows in its own money.

139
00:06:33,439 --> 00:06:35,500
A family borrows dollars it can't make.

140
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:40,033
This household borrows in dollars,
and the dollar is its own currency.

141
00:06:40,413 --> 00:06:43,229
So it can't run out of
dollars the way a family can.

142
00:06:43,609 --> 00:06:46,151
That's a big part of why
lenders keep saying yes.

143
00:06:46,531 --> 00:06:47,535
Question answered.

144
00:06:47,915 --> 00:06:48,917
But there's a catch.

145
00:06:49,297 --> 00:06:53,011
If a country makes too many new
dollars, each dollar buys less.

146
00:06:53,391 --> 00:06:54,421
That's inflation.

147
00:06:54,801 --> 00:06:55,601
Remember it.

148
00:06:55,956 --> 00:06:57,356
It comes back near the end.

149
00:06:58,086 --> 00:06:59,616
Thing two: it never retires.

150
00:06:59,996 --> 00:07:03,029
A family has to pay off its
debts within one lifetime.

151
00:07:03,409 --> 00:07:05,008
A country has no last payday.

152
00:07:05,388 --> 00:07:09,838
It can keep rolling old loans into new
ones for as long as lenders trust it.

153
00:07:10,218 --> 00:07:12,926
So the real question isn't,
can it pay it all back?

154
00:07:13,406 --> 00:07:15,500
It's, can it keep paying
the interest without

155
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,793
that envelope eating everything else?

156
00:07:18,523 --> 00:07:20,484
Thing three: it can give itself a raise.

157
00:07:20,864 --> 00:07:22,910
A family can't vote for a bigger salary.

158
00:07:23,290 --> 00:07:25,081
A country can change its tax rates.

159
00:07:25,461 --> 00:07:27,030
It can also cut its own bills.

160
00:07:27,410 --> 00:07:29,229
Both are hard, and both are choices.

161
00:07:29,609 --> 00:07:34,173
Which one is better is an argument for
voters, not for a cartoon roommate.

162
00:07:34,903 --> 00:07:37,284
And here's the difference
you can see in the data.

163
00:07:37,622 --> 00:07:40,920
In nineteen forty-six,
right after World War Two,

164
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:44,712
the debt held by the public was
bigger than the whole economy.

165
00:07:45,092 --> 00:07:47,846
Bigger than everything the
country produced in a year.

166
00:07:48,226 --> 00:07:51,219
By nineteen seventy-four,
it was under a quarter of it.

167
00:07:51,599 --> 00:07:52,932
And here's the strange part.

168
00:07:53,285 --> 00:07:54,714
The debt itself didn't shrink.

169
00:07:55,044 --> 00:07:56,015
It actually grew.

170
00:07:56,395 --> 00:07:59,698
The economy just grew much
faster, and prices rose too.

171
00:08:00,078 --> 00:08:00,952
The loan stayed.

172
00:08:01,332 --> 00:08:02,713
The paycheck got much bigger.

173
00:08:03,067 --> 00:08:05,827
A family can't grow its way
out of a loan like that.

174
00:08:06,207 --> 00:08:07,096
A country did.

175
00:08:07,826 --> 00:08:09,620
By the middle of twenty twenty-six,

176
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:12,951
that same measure was
about ninety-seven percent.

177
00:08:13,331 --> 00:08:15,558
Close to the size of
the whole economy again.

178
00:08:15,938 --> 00:08:19,520
So could this turn into a spiral,
where interest feeds borrowing,

179
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:21,671
and borrowing feeds more interest?

180
00:08:22,151 --> 00:08:25,210
It's a possibility, and some
economists worry about it.

181
00:08:25,590 --> 00:08:26,828
But there are real brakes.

182
00:08:27,171 --> 00:08:29,171
The debt is in the country's own currency.

183
00:08:29,536 --> 00:08:33,956
The debt comes due over years, not all
at once, so higher rates arrive slowly.

184
00:08:34,336 --> 00:08:37,680
And lenders around the world
still treat these bonds as

185
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:40,694
one of the safest places
to lend money in the world.

186
00:08:41,074 --> 00:08:42,878
A brake doesn't stop a car forever.

187
00:08:43,258 --> 00:08:44,169
It buys time.

188
00:08:44,549 --> 00:08:47,151
And time is what the three
possibilities are about.

189
00:08:47,881 --> 00:08:50,064
So how does a country
close a gap like this?

190
00:08:50,544 --> 00:08:51,606
Not with one trick.

191
00:08:51,986 --> 00:08:53,513
Here are three possibilities.

192
00:08:53,893 --> 00:08:55,246
This is not a forecast.

193
00:08:55,626 --> 00:08:58,548
It's what countries have done
before, and what it cost them.

194
00:08:58,928 --> 00:09:01,153
I'll put them on the
fridge as A, B, and C.

195
00:09:01,883 --> 00:09:03,713
Possibility A: grow the paycheck.

196
00:09:04,093 --> 00:09:06,600
If the economy grows faster than the debt,

197
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:11,330
the loan gets smaller next to the
income, even if nobody pays it down.

198
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:15,157
That's a big part of what
happened after nineteen forty-six.

199
00:09:15,537 --> 00:09:16,585
The catch is the rate.

200
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,702
Growth has to beat the interest rate.

201
00:09:19,044 --> 00:09:22,187
If the rate on the loan is higher
than the growth of the paycheck,

202
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:25,934
the loan wins, and envelope
five keeps getting fatter.

203
00:09:26,664 --> 00:09:28,974
Possibility B: change the envelopes.

204
00:09:29,354 --> 00:09:32,068
Raise taxes, cut spending,
or do some of both.

205
00:09:32,448 --> 00:09:33,877
Countries have done it before.

206
00:09:34,151 --> 00:09:37,641
The catch: every envelope on
this table belongs to somebody.

207
00:09:38,021 --> 00:09:40,201
Retirees, patients, soldiers, students.

208
00:09:40,581 --> 00:09:44,040
Every cut and every tax lands
on someone's kitchen table,

209
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:46,665
so these choices are slow and loud.

210
00:09:47,395 --> 00:09:49,535
Possibility C: let prices rise.

211
00:09:49,915 --> 00:09:52,140
Inflation makes old debt smaller,

212
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:55,785
because the dollars paid back buy
less than the dollars borrowed.

213
00:09:56,165 --> 00:09:58,269
That also helped after World War Two.

214
00:09:58,649 --> 00:10:03,020
In nineteen forty-six alone,
prices jumped about eighteen percent,

215
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:05,573
quietly shrinking what the debt was worth.

216
00:10:05,953 --> 00:10:07,220
The catch: it isn't free.

217
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,157
Rising prices hit everyone's grocery bill.

218
00:10:10,537 --> 00:10:11,943
And lenders aren't fools.

219
00:10:12,323 --> 00:10:15,990
If they expect inflation, they
ask for higher rates on new loans.

220
00:10:16,370 --> 00:10:19,824
So envelope five can come
right back, fatter than before.

221
00:10:20,554 --> 00:10:24,703
In real life, countries usually get
a mix of all three, plus some luck.

222
00:10:25,083 --> 00:10:27,892
Which mix comes next isn't
decided at this table.

223
00:10:28,272 --> 00:10:29,701
But you can now read the bill.

224
00:10:29,973 --> 00:10:31,558
So here's one question for you.

225
00:10:31,938 --> 00:10:35,190
A, B, or C: which one do you
think will do the most work?

226
00:10:35,670 --> 00:10:37,249
Just one letter in the comments.

227
00:10:37,979 --> 00:10:40,122
Let's go back to the
sentence from the start.

228
00:10:40,488 --> 00:10:41,866
A country isn't a family.

229
00:10:42,246 --> 00:10:47,062
It borrows in its own money, it never
retires, and it can give itself a raise.

230
00:10:47,442 --> 00:10:49,399
But the interest bill works the same way.

231
00:10:49,779 --> 00:10:52,025
Rate times balance, every single month.

232
00:10:52,405 --> 00:10:55,880
In our kitchen, that's about one
thousand fifty dollars a month,

233
00:10:56,180 --> 00:10:57,893
more than the whole military.

234
00:10:58,273 --> 00:10:59,948
Envelope five is small paper.

235
00:11:00,328 --> 00:11:02,464
It just keeps finding envelopes inside.

236
00:11:03,194 --> 00:11:07,460
There's one number that helps set the
price of many new loans in this kitchen,

237
00:11:07,820 --> 00:11:10,069
and of a lot of mortgages outside it.

238
00:11:10,449 --> 00:11:12,403
We saw it sitting close to five percent.

239
00:11:12,783 --> 00:11:15,660
Where that number comes from,
and who really moves it,

240
00:11:15,660 --> 00:11:18,145
is a story for another kitchen table.
